WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.314
Begin with assets, permitted actions and adversary
capabilities.

00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:10.313
The confidentiality, integrity and availability
goals turn a vague wish for security into

00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:11.189
requirements.

00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:15.772
A guard must identify a requester and check the
requested operation.

00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:22.378
The profile-query example shows why correct checks
also depend on safe interpretation of input and

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:24.669
complete coverage of access paths.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.972
Use the design principles to make concrete choices.

00:00:27.972 --> 00:00:34.259
Give a component only the rights it needs, deny by
default and keep decision logic understandable.

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:36.954
Plan what happens when permissions change.

00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:42.215
Combine isolation and independent checks while
keeping the secure workflow usable.

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:48.310
The backup example exposes the difference between
necessary access and convenient but excessive

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:48.951
authority.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.084
Imagine a laboratory booking service whose first
implementation sends a password with every

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:56.421
reservation request.

00:00:56.421 --> 00:01:03.040
Even over HTTPS, the reservation handler, diagnostic
logs and downstream integrations may encounter

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:03.842
that secret.

00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:09.859
Move password verification to a deliberate sign-in
boundary, then issue a separate session

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:12.199
credential for subsequent requests.

00:01:12.199 --> 00:01:18.751
This reduces repeated exposure of the long-lived
password, although the session credential remains

00:01:18.751 --> 00:01:20.355
valuable to an attacker.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.555
Now inspect a different failure: the account
database is copied.

00:01:24.555 --> 00:01:28.970
TLS cannot protect a database backup after it leaves
the connection.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:35.202
Dedicated password hashing with a unique salt
addresses this separate exposure by making offline

00:01:35.202 --> 00:01:36.501
guesses more costly.

00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:39.357
Neither mechanism substitutes for the other.

00:01:39.357 --> 00:01:45.785
Ask students to draw two attack paths, interception
during transport and theft of stored verifiers,

00:01:45.785 --> 00:01:48.252
and label the protection on each path.

00:01:48.252 --> 00:01:54.420
The architectural lesson is to match a mechanism to
the asset and threat it actually addresses.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.790
A volunteer signs in to a community archive and
receives an opaque session identifier.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:05.661
Later, an image-editing request carries that
identifier in a protected cookie or authorization

00:02:05.661 --> 00:02:06.098
header.

00:02:06.098 --> 00:02:12.031
The application looks up its associated account and
expiry, then separately checks whether that

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.779
account may edit this image.

00:02:13.779 --> 00:02:19.712
No account name supplied by the browser should
override the identity established by the session

00:02:19.712 --> 00:02:20.149
lookup.

00:02:20.149 --> 00:02:24.824
Authentication and resource authorization are
distinct steps in the trace.

00:02:24.824 --> 00:02:30.131
Suppose several application workers cache successful
session checks for two minutes.

00:02:30.131 --> 00:02:35.880
Disabling the account in the central store does not
instantly erase those cached decisions.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.882
The design needs a stated revocation delay,
invalidation mechanism or fresh check for sensitive

00:02:41.882 --> 00:02:42.387
actions.

00:02:42.387 --> 00:02:48.339
This makes the authentication service a consistency
and availability dependency as well as a

00:02:48.339 --> 00:02:49.568
security component.

00:02:49.568 --> 00:02:55.714
A request log should record useful operational
identifiers without retaining the bearer secret.

00:02:55.714 --> 00:03:00.501
Explain what a stolen session permits and how that
access eventually ends.
